pancake is a *codec-neutral* CapTP core: the session reasons in messages, and a
codec turns those messages into bytes and back (the
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) page sets up that seam). Two ship today.
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) settles the wire format as
[Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup), and pancake speaks Syrup wherever the
protocol calls for it; [Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) is the second codec, a
fixed-width, word-aligned alternative to Syrup's variable-length byte records.

[One message, two spellings. Syrup lays it out as variable-length byte records and Cap'n Proto as fixed 8-byte words; decode either and you get back the same message.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-codec.svg'))

## What a codec is, and is not

The session deals in messages, a deliver, a resolve, a promise, and never in
bytes. A codec only reads and writes bytes; it never decides what a message *means*
or whether it is *allowed*. That authority stays in the session, so changing codecs
changes the wire and never the behavior. Decode the same message through either
codec and you get the same borrowed view; encode through either and the bytes come
back equivalent. A build check holds that line: the deliver/resolve and
bootstrap-fetch scenarios produce identical observable traces on both codecs, even
though the two wire encodings look nothing alike.

## The two that ship

| Codec | Wire shape | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Syrup | length-prefixed, variable-length byte records | the default — and the only choice that reaches Goblins or any other OCapN peer, or when per-connection density matters |
| Cap'n Proto | fixed 8-byte-aligned words, every field at a known offset | two pancake peers, after measuring that fixed-offset access pays for the larger frames |

### Syrup

Syrup is the format [OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) settles on, so it is the
interop floor: CapTP 1.0 over Syrup is what pancake speaks by default and the only thing it
can speak to a [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) peer or any other
implementation. Its records are length-prefixed and variable-length, which keeps frames
compact and lets the decoder double as the stream framer, one parser finds a frame's
boundary and decodes it in the same pass.

Syrup also carries a second job no other codec takes over: it is the canonical form pancake
signs. A session's `op:start-session` location signature and every handoff certificate are
Ed25519 over canonical Syrup bytes, even when the conversation itself is riding Cap'n Proto.
So Syrup is the security substrate regardless of which codec moves the traffic.

Reading a Syrup value means walking its records in order. The engine's steady-state path
does exactly that, a single forward pass over a borrowed view, so it never pays to seek. A
byte record has no fixed offset, so jumping to element *i* of a list re-scans from the
start. That is the price of compactness, and it is why the hot path iterates rather than
indexes.

### Cap'n Proto

Cap'n Proto is the second codec: a layout of 8-byte words where every field sits at a known
offset, reached by following struct and list pointers. pancake's is hand-rolled against the
wire format with no schema compiler in the build, because it is a
*pancake-to-pancake dialect*: there is no third-party Cap'n Proto reader to satisfy, so a
generated schema would buy nothing. Both peers settle on it at the handshake
(`pairedWithCodec` in Zig, `pancake_peer_new_negotiated` over the C ABI, the matching idiom
in every binding), and the whole conversation rides it from there. It never reaches a
Goblins peer.

Fixed offsets are the point. Reaching field *i* is arithmetic, not a scan, so random access
is constant-time and the borrowed decode projection is cheap. That is the edge the
benchmark's faster Cap'n Proto decode reflects. The cost is size: padding every value to a
word and spending words on pointers makes frames larger, most visibly for the small messages
that dominate control traffic. Cap'n Proto can also carry a typed value where Syrup projects
a string — a typed abort reason, say — a small demonstration that the codec moves bytes while
the model keeps the meaning.

## The tradeoffs, plainly

Neither codec is "the fast one." They trade along four axes, and only the first is a hard
constraint.

- **Interop is decisive.** Syrup reaches every OCapN peer; Cap'n Proto reaches only another
  pancake that negotiated it. If anything on the far side is not pancake, the choice is made
  for you, and it is Syrup. This is the one axis you cannot measure your way around.
- **Wire size favors Syrup.** Variable-length records pack a small message into far fewer
  bytes than word-aligned padding plus pointers. At pancake's high-fan-out target, smaller
  frames mean less bandwidth and less in-flight memory per connection. The gap is widest for
  small messages and narrows as a payload's own bytes — a large blob, a long string — come
  to dominate the padding.
- **Access pattern favors Cap'n Proto.** Fixed offsets give constant-time random access and
  a cheap decode projection, where Syrup must scan its records in order. pancake's engine
  iterates forward on purpose, so the steady-state path does not lean on the difference, but
  a workload that seeks within large, field-heavy messages is where Cap'n Proto earns its
  frames back.
- **Signing is always Syrup.** Whichever codec carries the call, the handshake and handoff
  signatures are over canonical Syrup. Choosing Cap'n Proto changes the traffic encoding, not
  the security substrate.

Two things are *not* trades. Both codecs decode view-first — a borrowed projection over the
frame, with owned materialization only when the session retains a message — so zero-copy is
not a reason to pick one. And both answer to the same model: a value the model does not admit
is rejected with a typed error by either, never silently coerced, so switching codecs changes the wire and never the behavior.

## What the benchmark does and does not say

`zig build bench-codec` times one representative call, a method invocation with a small
mixed-type argument list, and the [Performance]($link.page('performance')) page shows the
result. On that message Syrup encodes a little faster and into a much smaller frame, and
Cap'n Proto decodes a little faster; the MB/s figures look lopsided only because Cap'n Proto
is moving several times as many bytes for the same message. Read per message, the two are
close.

That closeness is the headline for *typical* CapTP traffic, which is small control
messages. It is not a verdict that Cap'n Proto is pointless. Its advantage — constant-time
field access over large, structured, or partially read payloads — is a different message
shape than the benchmark measures. The takeaway is the inverse of the usual reflex: do not
reach for Cap'n Proto because it sounds faster, because for the messages OCapN sends most,
Syrup is smaller and no slower, and it is the only one that talks to anything but pancake.

## Which to reach for

Stay on **Syrup** unless you have a measured reason not to. It is the default, the interop
floor, and the signing substrate, and for ordinary traffic it is as quick and far more
compact. Reach for **Cap'n Proto** only on a pancake-to-pancake link whose payloads are large
or field-heavy enough that constant-time access pays for the bigger frames, and confirm it
with `zig build bench-codec` on the message shapes you actually send rather than this page's.

## Bring your own

The set is open on purpose. The two pancake ships are the two it needs today, but
the codec layer is a registry, not a fixed switch. A Zig consumer declares
`pub const pancake_codecs` in the root source file, binds a wire id to an
implementation, and the same core speaks it with no change to the session above. It
all resolves at compile time, so a third codec costs the dispatch nothing the two
built-ins did not.
